{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>But as for My servant Caleb, because, etc.</b> One needs to know why He mentioned only Caleb and not Joshua. Also the intent of His saying <b>because a different spirit was with him</b>, also His saying <b>and he fulfilled after Me</b>, also His saying <b>and I will bring him</b> with the addition of the letter vav, by decree of the verse." "<b>Indeed,</b> the meaning of the verse is as follows: <b>But as for My servant Caleb</b> — and the reason I call him My servant is עֵקֶב ('because'), meaning the reward that a different spirit was with him. For even though Joshua also did not anger HaShem with the spies, nevertheless this was due to Moses' prayer, which saved him from the evil inclination and its forces — namely the spies — so that they would not lead him astray; whereas Caleb entered the category of the danger of the evil inclination and its wicked company, and an evil spirit began to stir within him. The proof is that he went and prostrated himself upon the graves of the patriarchs. And that is what is meant by <b>a different spirit was with him</b>; yet nevertheless <b>and he fulfilled after Me</b> — meaning he completed and followed after the will of the Blessed One. And Scripture was precise in speaking in this manner, for the reason that a person has two counselors: an evil counselor and a good counselor to walk after HaShem, and he [Caleb] completed and followed after HaShem's portion. And this is along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Kiddushin 39b): one in whose path a transgression comes and who is saved from it — they give him reward like one who performs a mitzvah. And this was not the case with Joshua, for there was no different spirit with him to mislead him from the path of reason, since Moses restrained him. And there is power in the prayer of the righteous also for this consideration, in the mystical secret (II Samuel 23:3): ״צַדִּיק מוֹשֵׁל יִרְאַת אֱלֹהִים״ — and understand. Therefore Caleb merited being called the servant of HaShem like Moses our master, peace be upon him." "<b>And He further</b> adds to say — besides the aforementioned virtue — further: ״And I will bring him into the land where he went״, meaning, to pray for himself. And here He alluded to his having strengthened himself to the point that he fulfilled after HaShem. And the intent of this kindness is that not only did he not enter the category of the decree, but He promised him life — for even though he was forty years old then, and HaShem decreed that they would sit forty years in the wilderness, He promised him that he would live until he arrived there. And that is what He said: <b>and I will bring him</b> — he was thus promised life for forty years, something that was not done for any righteous person in the world." "<b>Or perhaps</b> it was for this reason that He prefaced by calling him His servant — to say that even though He informs him of the promise of life for forty years, he will not turn backward from the service of HaShem on account of his end being prolonged. And I have already explained (Shabbat 30b) in the matter of Hezekiah, to whom HaShem added fifteen years, where I raised a question regarding the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory, that HaShem replied to David when he asked of Him: ״Make me know, HaShem, my end, and the measure of my days,״ etc., and He said to him: It is a decree from before Me, that a person is not informed of his end, etc. — if so, why did HaShem inform Hezekiah? And I answered that precisely an end that a person brings with him — i.e., one that is decreed — as it is written (Job 14:5): ״אִם חֲרוּצִים יָמָיו״ — but additional days are not included under this decree. And HaShem wished to inform him, along the lines of what they say in the Gemara (Shabbat 10b) regarding the verse ״לָדַעַת כִּי אֲנִי ה׳ מְקַדִּשְׁכֶם״ — that one who gives a gift to his fellow must inform him; therefore HaShem makes known His kindness when He adds days to a person's days. And that is the meaning of ״and I will bring him״, etc. And so you will find that Scripture says regarding Caleb in passage 14: ״And Caleb said to him״, etc., ״and now behold HaShem has kept me alive, as He spoke״ — and these are our very words. And He further said <b>and his seed shall possess it</b>, to say that HaShem performed this kindness also for his seed." "<b>Further,</b> the verse may be explained along this path: ״עֵקֶב״ ('because') — because he did two good things. One: ״a different spirit was with him״ — meaning, he did not follow the counsel of the spies; and the second: ״and he fulfilled after Me״ — that he silenced the people and suppressed the words of the spies. And two things HaShem paid him in return: one — ״and I will bring him״, etc.; two — ״shall possess it״. And the reason He did not mention Joshua is that he merited by virtue of Moses' prayer and did not need to strengthen himself by his own power."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:30.295982035Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}